---
// Lesson 4.3a — Chase: The Aggro Radius
// TopDownMove: find by tag, chaseDistance, normalized pursuit.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.3a — Chase: The Aggro Radius</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Patrol is calm. Now the enemy notices you. One distance check turns
    a guard into a hunter — and normalization makes the hunter fair,
    because it moves at the same speed from every angle.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Read the chase half of EnemyController.cs:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private float chaseDistance = 5.0f;
private Transform playerTransform;

private void Start()
{
    GameObject player = GameObject.FindWithTag("Player");
    if (player != null)
    {
        playerTransform = player.transform;
    }
    ...
}

private void TopDownMove()
{
    if (playerTransform == null) return;

    float dist = Vector3.Distance(transform.position, playerTransform.position);

    if (dist &lt; chaseDistance)
    {
        Vector3 dir = (playerTransform.position - transform.position).normalized;
        transform.Translate(dir * speed * Time.deltaTime);
        ...
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Predict: you walk circles around the enemy at exactly the
        radius's edge. Does it lurch diagonally faster than straight —
        remember the diagonal tinker from
        <a href="1.5a.html">1.5a</a>? What did <code>.normalized</code>
        do about that, and what would the chase feel like without it?
        (Fix the memory first, then re-answer.)
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The enemy finds the player ONCE in Start and holds the
            reference in playerTransform. Why is that safer than
            calling FindWithTag every frame — and when would the
            cached transform become a null disaster (hint: scene
            changes, like the cave door in <a href="5.3a.html">5.3a</a>)?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — aim the pursuit vector.</strong> The whole hunt
    is one line of arithmetic:
    <code>(playerTransform.position - transform.position)</code> is
    the arrow FROM the enemy TO you. Minus order matters — subtract
    backwards and the enemy flees. Try it: flip the subtraction, run
    the game, watch your enemy become a coward. Restore.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — normalize, seen.</strong> With
    <code>.normalized</code>: walking toward you from any angle, the
    enemy covers the same distance per second. Without it (delete the
    call, keep everything else): the closer it gets, the SHORTER the
    vector, the slower it closes in — an asymptote of menace. That is
    why the turret's projectile used normalized in
    <a href="3.2a.html">3.2a</a>: constant bullet speed from every
    firing angle.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — two thresholds, one brain.</strong> The enemy
    now has patrolDistance (3) AND chaseDistance (5). Both use the
    same Distance call in different modes. Which wins when you are
    inside BOTH — and what does the enemy do in the moment you step
    from 5.1 to 4.9? Watch the flip: it is a state change with no if
    for "switch to chase" — the chase IS the if.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The broken hunter</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Delete the null check in Start and remove the Player
                from the scene. What crashes — and where exactly
                (which line, which method)?</li>
            <li>Set chaseDistance to 100. Where does "chasing" stop
                being a behavior and start being teleport-adjacent —
                and why is 100 really "there is no patrol
                anymore"?</li>
            <li>Set the enemy's speed HIGHER than the player's xSpeed.
                Can the player escape at all — and what does that do
                to the game's fairness contract?</li>
            <li>Return early: <code>if (playerTransform == null)
                return;</code>. Why must this run BEFORE the Distance
                call — what would Unity say otherwise?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Aggro design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Set chaseDistance slightly LARGER than the turret's
                range and place them together. What combined kill zone
                did you design, and where is the one safe line?</li>
            <li>Give the chase a memory: once you are seen, the enemy
                keeps chasing even outside the radius until you reach
                12 away. What new field does that need — and does
                the AP exam's word "state" apply to it?</li>
            <li>Hard: LOS — should walls stop the chase? What Unity
                feature would you have to borrow (hint: raycast),
                and why is that too expensive to run 60×/second?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Write the pursuit: subtraction → normalize → translate, in that order</li>
            <li>Explain what normalized buys (constant speed) and show the bug without it</li>
            <li>Explain the cached playerTransform and its null danger</li>
            <li>Design an aggro radius that reads as fair to the player</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/index.html">Unit 4: The Enemy</a> — chase and pursuit math</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/enemy-state-machines.html">Enemy State Machines</a> — chase as a state</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — normalized, FindWithTag</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="4.4a.html">4.4a — Attack: Close-Range Danger</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="4.3a" title="4.3a Chase: The Aggro Radius — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
